import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.Stack;
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
class Solution {
    record Price(int price, int day) {}

    public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {
        int profit = 0;
        PriorityQueue<Price> minPrices = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(Price::price));
        PriorityQueue<Price> maxPrices = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(Price::price).reversed());

        for (int i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {
            minPrices.add(new Price(prices[i], i));
            maxPrices.add(new Price(prices[i], i));
        }

        while (!minPrices.isEmpty()) {
            Price currPrice = minPrices.poll();

            ArrayDeque<Price> placeholderPrices = new ArrayDeque<>();
            while (!maxPrices.isEmpty()) {
                Price maxPrice = maxPrices.poll();
                placeholderPrices.add(maxPrice);

                if (currPrice.day() < maxPrice.day())

            }

        }



        return profit;
    }
}

javaHelp.answer:
In Java 25, declare the two components in parentheses:

```java
record Pair(int first, int second) {}

Pair p = new Pair(3, 7);
int a = p.first();
int b = p.second();
```

The constructor and component accessor methods are generated automatically ([JLS §8.10](https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/jls/se25/html/jls-8.html#jls-8.10)).

Pass a comparator that selects the record component you want to order by. Generic Java 25 example:

```java
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

record Entry(int score, int id) {}

PriorityQueue<Entry> heap =
    new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(Entry::score));
```

[`comparingInt`](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Comparator.html#comparingInt(java.util.function.ToIntFunction)) uses `score()` as the comparison key. The entry with the **smallest score** is at the [queue’s head](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/util/PriorityQueue.html). To order by the other component instead, use `Entry::id`.

In Java 25, use an ascending comparator for a **min-heap**, and reverse it for a **max-heap**:

```java
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

record Entry(int score, int id) {}

Comparator<Entry> byScore = Comparator.comparingInt(Entry::score);
PriorityQueue<Entry> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(byScore);
PriorityQueue<Entry> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(byScore.reversed());
```

[`comparingInt`](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Comparator.html#comparingInt(java.util.function.ToIntFunction)) selects the comparison key; [`reversed()`](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Comparator.html#reversed()) reverses the ordering. A [`PriorityQueue`](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/util/PriorityQueue.html) places the least element under that ordering at its head.

[`peek()`](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/util/PriorityQueue.html#peek()) returns an **`Entry`**, not its score: one with the smallest score for `minHeap`, or largest for `maxHeap`, without removing it. It returns `null` when empty.

